1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,403
Can a model get top marks on labels that are pure noise?

2
00:00:09,403 --> 00:00:10,033
Watch this.

3
00:00:10,033 --> 00:00:15,411
We have forty patients with five scans each, and every patient's label comes from a coin flip.

4
00:00:15,411 --> 00:00:19,015
On the left we shuffle all the scans and hold out half of them,

5
00:00:19,015 --> 00:00:24,100
and on the right we hold out half of the patients instead.

6
00:00:24,100 --> 00:00:30,615
Now take the laziest model there is, where each test scan just copies the label of the closest training scan.

7
00:00:30,615 --> 00:00:36,951
On the left, the closest scan belongs to the same patient, so it scores one hundred percent on coin flips.

8
00:00:36,951 --> 00:00:39,521
Split by patient and the trick falls apart.

9
00:00:39,521 --> 00:00:45,966
We get fifty-eight percent, which is about chance, and that's the honest number.

10
00:00:45,966 --> 00:00:51,945
Here's a sneakier version of the same problem, with two hospitals and a hundred chest scans each.

11
00:00:51,945 --> 00:00:56,752
At the first one thirty-four patients are sick, and at the second only one is.

12
00:00:56,752 --> 00:01:00,512
Now ignore the pixels and guess from the hospital name alone.

13
00:01:00,512 --> 00:01:07,800
The area under the ROC curve, where one half is chance, comes out at zero point seven nine.

14
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:13,121
And this really happened, to a published pneumonia detector built on a convolutional network.

15
00:01:13,121 --> 00:01:16,783
It scored zero point nine three on scans from its own hospitals.

16
00:01:16,783 --> 00:01:19,644
At a new hospital it fell to zero point eight two.

17
00:01:19,644 --> 00:01:24,200
It was the same model and the same disease, just in a different building.

18
00:01:24,200 --> 00:01:26,439
And what about the hospital name alone?

19
00:01:26,439 --> 00:01:32,584
That scores zero point eight six on the pooled test set, because the sick rates differed from site to site.

20
00:01:32,584 --> 00:01:38,499
So the network was reading scanner quirks, patient positioning, and stamped text, instead of the lungs.

21
00:01:38,499 --> 00:01:42,691
A random split can't catch this, because the shortcut sits on both sides.

22
00:01:42,691 --> 00:01:46,300
You have to hold out the hospital.

23
00:01:46,300 --> 00:01:50,119
So the rule is to hold out whatever will be new in deployment.

24
00:01:50,119 --> 00:01:54,739
That might be a patient, a document, a time period, or a molecule scaffold.

25
00:01:54,739 --> 00:02:01,866
But match deployment and no more, because a test set that differs in unrelated ways misleads you just as much.

26
00:02:01,866 --> 00:02:07,119
And none of this is rare, because surveys across many fields keep finding leakage like this,

27
00:02:07,119 --> 00:02:11,133
and it keeps inflating published results.

28
00:02:11,133 --> 00:02:14,416
Before building any model, there are four things to check.

29
00:02:14,416 --> 00:02:17,982
A pattern has to exist, and it has to matter for some decision.

30
00:02:17,982 --> 00:02:23,190
It also has to show up in the features we record, and a model has to be able to pull it out.

31
00:02:23,190 --> 00:02:31,766
The hospital shortcut passed the last three checks and still missed the point, because it wasn't medicine.

32
00:02:31,766 --> 00:02:35,546
Now let's zoom out for a moment and name the kinds of learning.

33
00:02:35,546 --> 00:02:41,006
Everything so far was supervised learning, where labels drive regression or classification.

34
00:02:41,006 --> 00:02:46,826
Take the labels away and you're finding structure instead, with embeddings or density estimation.

35
00:02:46,826 --> 00:02:51,806
And foundation models learn from broad data first and then get prompted or adapted,

36
00:02:51,806 --> 00:02:56,000
which is the same ideas at a bigger scale.

37
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,291
So here's the whole unit one more time, in five moves.

38
00:02:59,291 --> 00:03:01,730
Samples alone don't pin down a function.

39
00:03:01,730 --> 00:03:05,692
Ramps are flexible pieces, and a layer runs many of them at once.

40
00:03:05,692 --> 00:03:13,233
What we train on isn't what we actually want, and an honest test holds out exactly what will be new.

